    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "51a72f11_5f2cbd74",
        "filename": "MODULE.bazel",
        "patchSetId": 43
      },
      "lineNbr": 63,
      "author": {
        "id": 1011323
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-01-09T21:12:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I see your point, and I agree that it would be ideal if we didn’t have to add extra complexity on our side just to reduce overly verbose warnings.\n\nI looked into this a bit more and found that the underlying issue is actually already addressed upstream in `rules_jvm_external`. There is a fix that restores the repository ordering to follow the order specified in Starlark (instead of sorting repositories lexicographically), which is exactly what we relied on before:\n\nhttps://github.com/bazel-contrib/rules_jvm_external/pull/1447\n\nThe change itself is correct, but the corresponding prebuilt\n`private/tools/prebuilt/lock_file_converter_deploy.jar` has not been released yet. \n\nBecause of that, the fix does not take effect in the currently published versions.\nTo verify this, I created a small fork of rules_jvm_external that only updates that prebuilt JAR, and consumed it via:\n\n```python\n# TODO(davido): Remove the fork after a fixed version of\n# private/tools/prebuilt/lock_file_converter_deploy.jar\n# is released:\n# https://github.com/davido/rules_jvm_external/commit/2275b9c4ccc2f7ece26b8a4dffad591f87c0cff7\n# See also: https://github.com/bazel-contrib/rules_jvm_external/pull/1447\narchive_override(\n    module_name \u003d \"rules_jvm_external\",\n    integrity \u003d \"sha256-HpFDeE1A6CnXsUviTNqoNeM/gyw8A0M6xgKHUeTM8Cw\u003d\",\n    strip_prefix \u003d \"rules_jvm_external-2275b9c4ccc2f7ece26b8a4dffad591f87c0cff7\",\n    urls \u003d [\n        \"https://github.com/davido/rules_jvm_external/archive/2275b9c4ccc2f7ece26b8a4dffad591f87c0cff7.tar.gz\",\n    ],\n)\n```\n\nWith this in place, re-pinning preserves the repository order from MODULE.bazel, and the resolver behaves exactly as you described: Maven Central is tried first, and the Google-hosted repositories are only queried when needed.\n\nThat already removes the large wave of misleading 404s. What remains are a small number of warnings (in our case about 12) for artifacts that really only exist in the Google storage bucket.\n\nIt would indeed be nice if those could be suppressed or downgraded to debug-level logging, but at least now they reflect genuine fallbacks rather than a broken resolution order.\n\nSo I think we’re mostly aligned here: once the upstream fix (including the prebuilt JAR) is released, the behavior should be much cleaner without us needing extra workarounds.",
      "parentUuid": "91c82050_1a0cb5f9",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 63,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 63,
        "endChar": 25
